config FMT
	bool "fmt: integer conversions, and printf into io buffers"
	default y
	select STATUS
	select IO
	help
	  Integers to decimal and hex text in a caller's scratch array, and
	  a printf subset that formats into an io buffer (el_buf) or a
	  buffered writer, keeping a number whole or absent. No floating
	  point. Freestanding: needs only memcpy().

choice FMT_PRINTF
	prompt "fmt: what printf accepts"
	default FMT_PRINTF_BUILTIN
	depends on FMT
	help
	  The portable subset of printf (elemental/fmt_printf.h) is built-in
	  code under either option, with identical output. The C library
	  option also accepts the rest of C's numeric conversions, one at a
	  time through snprintf(). A format correct under it is a contract
	  violation under the built-in option
	  (docs/adr/0016-private-choices-that-widen.md). Code that must build
	  against any configuration uses the portable subset only.

config FMT_PRINTF_BUILTIN
	bool "Built-in: the portable subset"
	help
	  d i u x X c s % with the 0 flag, decimal widths, a precision on
	  %s, and the length modifiers <inttypes.h> needs. No C library
	  beyond memcpy(), and interrupt-safe into a fixed buffer. The
	  right choice unless the image needs floats, %p or printf's other
	  flags through Elemental's truncation rule.

config FMT_PRINTF_LIBC
	bool "The C library's snprintf() for everything else"
	depends on PLATFORM_SNPRINTF
	help
	  Also accepts every other specification of d i u x X o f F e E g G
	  a A p (flags, precision, '*', j t L), formatted one conversion at
	  a time by snprintf() and entered whole or not at all, and %s and
	  %c with '-' or a '*' width. Floats are whatever the C library
	  writes. For an image that links the C library's printf anyway, or
	  needs floats or %p. Formats in the portable subset still never
	  reach snprintf(). Needs snprintf() from the C library.

endchoice
